NXR-133|Optional simple FPGA DMA driver|DMA|The BSP shall optionally support one selected FPGA DMA engine after interrupt and IPC prerequisites, with one active transfer per channel and fixed aligned buffers.|Provide bulk transfer without growing the mandatory nano kernel.|Driver serializes thread callers; interrupt completion signals a waiter; invalid alignment, length or busy channel fails unchanged; scatter/gather, cyclic transfers and automatic exit cancellation remain excluded initially; disabled DMA adds no driver storage.|Future selected-engine transfer and configuration qualification.|NXR-115,NXR-118,NXR-119,NXR-131|Proposed RTOS_DMA optional, off in nano; actual engine selected by BSP.|Static channel state and explicit buffer capacities/alignment; report exact target costs.|docs/device-ipc-review.rst,docs/drivers.rst|Roadmap now includes a later simple DMA BSP driver; earlier deferral still applies to the initial IPC/device phase.
NXR-134|DMA buffer ownership and quiescence|DMA|The DMA driver shall retain exclusive hardware buffer ownership until verified completion or proven quiescence and quarantine uncertain transfers after timeout or owner exit.|Prevent memory corruption from late hardware writes.|Timeout alone never releases a buffer; recovery proves engine stop/reset before reuse; stale completion cannot release a newer transfer; errors and quarantine are observable; no unbounded abort wait is permitted.|Future timeout, delayed completion, reset and owner-exit fault scenarios.|NXR-133,NXR-120,NXR-129|Per-engine bounded completion/reset policy; no generic cancellation promise.|Fixed ownership/generation and quarantine state; retained buffers remain charged to static budget.|docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Later DMA work must establish a hardware-specific stop contract before any timed-out buffer can be reused.
NXR-135|DMA address and cache contract|DMA|The BSP shall define DMA-reachable bus address ranges, width, alignment, cache maintenance and ordering independently from CPU virtual or physical pointer representation.|Make DMA portable across FPGA and ARM memory systems.|Overflowing or unreachable ranges fail before launch; cache operations respect complete owned cache lines; direction and barriers are explicit; unsupported coherency or addressing fails configuration; no IOMMU is assumed.|Future BSP mapping review and coherency qualification.|NXR-117,NXR-133|Selected engine and memory-region manifest; coherent or maintained buffers explicit.|Static aligned regions and mapping metadata; cache-line padding reported.|docs/device-ipc-review.rst,docs/drivers.rst|DMA addresses cannot be inferred from C++ pointers; real BSP memory topology is still unknown.
NXR-136|Conditional SpaceVPX PCIe transport|PCIe|The BSP plan shall treat PCIe over SpaceVPX as a conditional optional transport with explicit endpoint or root-port scope and a required hardware integration manifest.|Allow FPGA inter-board communication without assuming topology or compliance.|Manifest records IP/revision, role, generation, lanes, slot profile, peer, BAR/windows, address width, IRQ, reset and performance budgets; endpoint registers may precede DMA; root-port enumeration is separately scoped; no SpaceVPX qualification is implied.|Future hardware selection and interface design review.|NXR-114,NXR-117,NXR-131|Proposed RTOS_PCIE off by default; role and IP remain undecided.|Declared BAR/control storage only; root-port tables and DMA buffers separately budgeted.|docs/device-ipc-review.rst|Possible SpaceVPX PCIe communication is recorded as conditional architecture work, not an implemented or selected board target.
NXR-137|Versioned peer communication protocol|PCIe|The PCIe transport shall define a versioned fixed-width register/mailbox protocol with explicit endian, bounds, ordering, ownership and backpressure before bulk DMA is enabled.|Keep peer communication independent of compiler layout and local addresses.|Incompatible versions fail safely; lengths and ranges validate without overflow; native pointers and C++ layouts never cross the link; reset generations reject stale responses; synchronization follows actual bridge semantics.|Future peer conformance and malformed-message scenarios.|NXR-136,NXR-118|Static mailbox depth and message bounds; register-only mode supported where hardware permits.|Fixed mailbox storage and protocol state; no mandatory DMA buffers.|docs/device-ipc-review.rst|A PCIe bridge does not supply an application messaging protocol; wire layout and peer contracts remain to be designed.
NXR-138|PCIe fault and DMA window isolation|PCIe|The PCIe BSP shall bound accessible peer and local windows and preserve ownership through link loss, reset and uncertain outstanding DMA until quiescence is established.|Contain malformed peer requests and late transactions.|Reject out-of-window access before submission; link loss faults pending work; buffers remain reserved until hardware-specific recovery proves safety; reconnect rejects stale generations; IRQ and posted-write ordering follow selected IP; fault counters are exposed.|Future link-drop, reset, range-validation and late-write qualification.|NXR-134,NXR-135,NXR-136,NXR-137|Board-specific windows, interrupt mechanism and reset/quiescence policy.|Static window, request-generation and health records; quarantine budget explicit.|docs/device-ipc-review.rst|PCIe reset and link loss require a selected hardware contract; software timeouts and address checks alone do not establish isolation.

